WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.570
INTRO:
Time and time again, we’ve seen how oil

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:05.479
can transform an entire country.

00:00:05.479 --> 00:00:09.950
Just a hundred years ago, for example, the
middle east was more or less just a desert

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:10.950
wasteland.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:15.770
But, today, many of these countries have become
more modern than even western countries.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.940
I mean, when your police are using lambhorghinis
and mercedes for patrol, clearly you’ve

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:21.940
got money.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.660
But, ironically, a few of the countries with
the most amount of oil in the entire world

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.170
don’t actually benefit from it all that
much.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:34.590
One of these countries is Canada which has
170 billion barrels of oil.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.320
This makes them the country the third largest
oil reserves in the entire world.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.670
The only countries that have more oil are
Saudi Arabia and Venezuela.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.200
Saudi Arabia literally has a $2 trillion oil
company, so clearly they’re using their

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.309
resources to the max.

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:55.030
Meanwhile, Venezuela is a third world country
filled with hyperinflation and corruption,

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.309
so it’s not surprising that they haven’t
leveraged their resources.

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:01.420
But, what about Canada?

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:06.250
Canada is a well respected western country
with plenty of intelligent and ambitious people.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:11.570
Yet, before the pandemic screwed up oil prices,
oil and gas only accounted for $128 billion

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.690
worth of Canda’s GDP.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.220
For perspective, there were years where Saudi
Aramco’s profits alone were nearly $128

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.220
billion.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:25.780
So, why doesn’t Canada exploit their gigantic
oil reserves?

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.759
ECONOMICAL DISABILITY:
One of the biggest challenges that Canda has

00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:33.790
faced in terms of oil is competing in the
global market.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:39.299
You see, there’s several different types
of oil and not all oils are created equal.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:43.680
Onshore Middle Eastern oil for example is
one of the easiest to extract and refine.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.900
Meanwhile, the type of oil Canda has, Oil
Sands, is one of the hardest to extract and

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:49.900
refine.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.909
The reason for this is that oil sands aren’t
even a liquid form of oil.

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:58.610
Oil sands are actually a combination of oil,
sand, clay, and water that create this tar

00:01:58.610 --> 00:01:59.670
like substance.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:03.630
So, you can’t just setup a bunch of oil
pumps and ride these to the moon.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.979
Instead, you have to engage in one of two
complex mining and separation processes.

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:12.489
For deposits found on the surface, the oil
sands first have be strip mined.

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:17.359
Then, they have to be run through hot water
to isolate a substance called bitumen.

00:02:17.359 --> 00:02:20.519
From there, you have to convert the bitumen
into oil.

00:02:20.519 --> 00:02:25.719
This process itself is already quite complex
and expensive, but the worst part is that

00:02:25.719 --> 00:02:29.239
this process can only be applied to land based
deposits.

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:32.370
And 80% of oil sands are not land based.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.859
Usually, the majority of oil sands are buried
more the 75 meters under the ground which

00:02:36.859 --> 00:02:39.049
requires in situ production.

00:02:39.049 --> 00:02:42.519
This is when you pump bunch of hot steam into
the ground to separate the bitumen from the

00:02:42.519 --> 00:02:43.859
oil sands.

00:02:43.859 --> 00:02:47.870
From there, an extraction well will collect
the isolated bitumen which can then be converted

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.319
into crude oil.

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:53.639
As you would guess, thanks to the complexity
of this process, oil sand production is naturally

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:56.090
way more expensive than traditional oil drilling.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.430
But, that’s just the first problem for Canada.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.249
Given that Canada is a developed Western country,
they actually have standards when it comes

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.969
to wages and working conditions.

00:03:04.969 --> 00:03:10.760
In Alberta, for example, the average wage
for oil and gas workers is 36.52 CAD per hour

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.189
which works out roughly 75,000 CAD per year.

00:03:14.189 --> 00:03:20.129
Even if we look at the lowest 5% of oil workers
in Alberta, they’re paid 15 CAD per hour

00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:23.099
or 31,200 CAD per year.

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:27.650
In terms of US dollars, this is equivalent
to roughly $24,000 per year.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:32.659
Now, most westerners probably wouldn’t classify
this as a high salary per se, but compared

00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:35.389
to Saudi Arabian standards, this is insanely
high.

00:03:35.389 --> 00:03:41.659
The average laborer in Saudi Arabia only makes
36,000 riyals per year or about $9,500 per

00:03:41.659 --> 00:03:42.659
year.

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:47.040
This means that Saudi Aramco can hire 2 and
half workers for every employee Alberta hires.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.139
Also, I suspect that Alberta likely has better
benefits and fewer working hours per week.

00:03:52.139 --> 00:03:56.379
So, the ratio is even more skewed in Saudi
Arabia’s favor.

00:03:56.379 --> 00:04:00.849
Considering all this, it’s simply impossible
for Canada to compete in terms of price.

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:05.150
And this is why Canadian oil is the most expensive
in the world.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:09.620
The average barrel of oil from Canada has
a breakeven cost of $74 per barrel.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:14.400
Meanwhile, the average barrel of oil from
the Middle East has a breakeven cost of $29

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:15.400
per barrel.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.459
With these numbers, it’s not even a competition.

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:22.340
Canada would have to lose $45 per barrel just
to match Middle Eastern prices.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:27.470
Meanwhile, the Middle East only has to lose
$29 per barrel to offer their oil for free.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:31.270
With such a massive pricing disadvantage,
it’s no wonder why Canada’s oil industry

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.139
isn’t that lucrative.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:39.050
LOCATION LOCATION LOCATION:
While Canada can never reach the profit margins

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.389
reached by the Middle East, this doesn't mean
that can never turn a profit.

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:47.419
You see, oil prices are oftentimes way higher
than $74 per barrel.

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:53.840
Between 2004 and 2014, for example, oil prices
were regularly over $100 per barrel.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:59.860
So, Canada had plenty of time to run a profitable
operation, but this never really came to fruition

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.370
due to their location.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:05.710
The Middle East is literally located in the
perfect place to serve a global market.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:07.800
On one side, they have Asia.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.770
On another side, they have Europe.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.050
On another side, they have Africa.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.539
And on their last side, they have the ocean.

00:05:14.539 --> 00:05:17.870
This gives them a massive advantage when it
comes to building robust freight networks,

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.949
trucking networks, railroads, pipelines, and
so on.

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:25.139
Meanwhile, Canada has the United States and
that’s pretty much it.

00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:29.319
If they want to export oil to any other country,
they’re only option is to use the sea.

00:05:29.319 --> 00:05:34.290
And Canada has basically completely neglected
this option given that 99% of their oil ends

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.729
up in the North American market.

00:05:35.729 --> 00:05:39.819
Honestly, there doesn’t seem to be any clear
reason as to why they haven’t grown their

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:41.180
freight operation.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.740
The main bottleneck seems to be that their
oil infrastructure is heavily underdeveloped

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:50.159
which has made it extremely difficult to transport
oil from Alberta to the pacific coast.

00:05:50.159 --> 00:05:55.160
There have been a few projects that have attempted
to solve this issue but, more times than not,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.630
these projects were plagued with issues and
setbacks.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.460
Take the Trans Mountain Pipeline for example.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.889
This project was designed to help transport
oil from Alberta to the coast of British Columbia

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:07.889
and it was commissioned way back in 1951.

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:11.349
Work on this project progressed pretty quickly
and the Candians had the pipeline up and running

00:06:11.349 --> 00:06:13.300
by 1953.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:19.039
But to this day, this pipeline is the only
pipeline that connects these to areas.

00:06:19.039 --> 00:06:23.830
For nearly 10 years now, Canada has been trying
to add a parallel pipeline to this project,

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:28.500
but due to politics and environmentalists,
this project has been constantly delayed.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:33.419
The latest delay has pushed the completion
date into Q3 of 2023, but who knows if that’ll

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:34.909
actually play out.

00:06:34.909 --> 00:06:40.069
In the meantime, Canada’s oil industry is
basically 100% dependent on the United States.

00:06:40.069 --> 00:06:44.520
Fortunately, the US is the world’s largest
oil consumer, so Canada has plenty of demand

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.319
for their oil, but this also puts them in
a super awkward position.

00:06:49.319 --> 00:06:54.300
The US is well aware that they can single
handedly make or break Canada’s oil industry,

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.870
so they’re not exactly motivated to play
nice.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:00.999
In fact, they prefer to essentially black
mail Canada into giving them a significant

00:07:00.999 --> 00:07:03.310
discount of up to $20 per barrel.

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:08.410
Factoring in this discount, Canada’s breakeven
price for oil isn’t just $74 per barrel,

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.490
but more like $94 per barrel.

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:15.689
Really, they need oil to be over $100 per
barrel to have any shot at making a profit.

00:07:15.689 --> 00:07:19.770
Hopefully, the Trans Mountain Expansion project
will be completed soon and Canada to start

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.620
exporting to Asia as well.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:26.099
But, in the meantime, Canada is not only cursed
by the type of their oil, but also by their

00:07:26.099 --> 00:07:29.379
location and dependence on the US.

00:07:29.379 --> 00:07:34.490
ENVIRONMENTAL HAVOC:
At this point, you might be thinking that

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:40.860
things can’t get any worse for Canada, but
the reality is that they can and they have.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.169
Aside from having fight this massive uphill
battles with oil sands and their location,

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:48.199
Canada also has to put up a serious fight
with environmentalists.

00:07:48.199 --> 00:07:53.439
Now, it’s natural for any fossil fuel based
operation to gain some opposition, but oil

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:57.660
sands garner especially high opposition because
they’re especially harmful to the environment.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:03.240
You see, the process of converting oil sands
to usable oil is not only a long, expensive,

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:07.509
and complex process, but also a water and
energy intensive process.

00:08:07.509 --> 00:08:12.099
To make things worse, the process doesn’t
exactly release the kindest byproducts either.

00:08:12.099 --> 00:08:17.199
In fact, oil sand extraction emits up to three
times more global warming pollution than traditional

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:18.279
crude oil extraction.

00:08:18.279 --> 00:08:21.250
It’s not just air based pollution either.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:26.300
Oil sand production not only depletes freshwater
sources, but it also creates gigantic ponds

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.990
of toxic waste.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:31.960
Considering this, it’s not surprising that
environmentalists are all over this.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:35.500
In fact, environmentalists are one of the
main reasons that the Trans Mountain Expansion

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.800
project has had so many delays.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:42.560
They argue that the original pipeline itself
is already extremely destructive, so it makes

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.149
no sense to build yet another pipeline.

00:08:45.149 --> 00:08:48.000
And honestly, they do have a point.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:53.100
Since 1961, Trans Mountain has reported 84
oil spills which is more than 1 spill every

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.100
single year.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.880
Also, it’s not just the environment that’s
at stake either.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.800
There have already been red flags that sand
oil production is taking a toll on local residents.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:06.370
Researchers have scientifically proven that
areas with oil sand production have been correlated

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.760
with higher cancer rates.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:14.190
Now of course, correlation does not mean causation,
but honestly the link is clear as day.

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:17.389
Taking a bigger picture view, it’s not just
Canada’s environment that would be hurt

00:09:17.389 --> 00:09:18.810
by oil production.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:22.350
The goal of producing oil is to sell it to
countries that need it.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:26.730
And when these countries use said oil, they’ll
be polluting their own ecosystems as well.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:30.370
Now of course, you could make the argument
that if these countries didn’t buy from

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:33.980
Canada, they’ll just buy from somewhere
else and that’s absolutely true.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:38.990
But, from an ethical perspective, it wouldn’t
be Canada thats supporting said pollution.

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:42.160
Now, you might be saying, how does that change
anything?

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:46.620
Well, the thing to note is that Canada doesn't
need to sell oil to do well.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.480
They’re already doing well.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:52.300
The same however cannot be said most of the
middle eastern countries that are like 90%

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.510
dependent on oil revenue.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:57.560
So, it makes sense to leave the polluting
to countries that have to do it and avoid

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:01.329
polluting yourself especially when you don’t
have to.

00:10:01.329 --> 00:10:07.490
A POTENTIAL OPPURTUNITY:
In the end, Canada doesn’t just have one

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:11.529
of the world’s largest oil reserves, but
also one of the world’s most problematic

00:10:11.529 --> 00:10:12.800
oil reserves.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:17.310
Oil sands are expensive to extract, they’re
super harmful for the environment, and Canada’s

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:20.180
doesn’t have the infrastructure transport
large amounts of oil.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:24.550
Despite all these obstacles, it seems like
Canada may actually have an opportunity to

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:27.660
grow their oil industry substantially in the
coming years thanks to Russia.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:31.850
As y’all probably already know, sanctioning
off Russian oil has created a massive oil

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:33.980
shortage across the world.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:37.899
This is one of the main reasons that oil prices
rocketed to over $100 per barrel just a few

00:10:37.899 --> 00:10:39.050
months ago.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:42.980
Also, it should be noted that much of Europe
hasn’t even stopped consuming Russian oil

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:43.980
yet.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:48.720
Their plan is to stop by 2023, so as Europe
continues to pull back from Russia, they’re

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.009
demand for other sources of oil will simply
grow.

00:10:51.009 --> 00:10:54.509
In fact, Canada’s oil production is not
enough to offset Russia’s.

00:10:54.509 --> 00:10:57.529
So, Canada should have plenty of demand for
their oil.

00:10:57.529 --> 00:11:02.120
But, if Canada isn’t able to build up their
oil and freight infrastructure, all of that

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.120
demand will mean nothing for Canada.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:08.610
On the other hand, many feel that this is
actually the best case scenario for Canada.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:12.820
They feel that there is no reason for Canada
to get more involved in the oil game.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:16.090
Just because they have oil doesn't mean they
have to ruin their ecosystem, put communities

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.329
at risk, and become more dependent on oil
revenue.

00:11:19.329 --> 00:11:23.610
Canada already has one of the largest economies
in the world and one of the happiest populations

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:24.610
in the world.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:29.000
So, why put all of that at risk just for a
bit of oil money.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.980
Do you guys think Canada should leverage this
opportunity with Russia?

